  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin
    
    A shard of crystallized power born from the Ofundis∴flux, the catastrophic surge of ancient energy that tore through the Nightshelf mining complex of Sleek☽ Caemomot during the Frozen Peace. The Shadow-glass is obsidian-black on its outer shell, shot through with veins of captured fire-light visible only in darkness — in daylight it appears inert, a quality that has contributed to its repeated mishandling by those who mistake dormancy for safety. The artifact radiates a silence reportedly deep enough to penetrate surrounding stone and reach beneath the frozen sea.
    
    Three factions claimed custodianship through theft the moment it crystallized. This established a pattern.
    
    ## Chain of Custody
    
    | Holder | Circumstance of Acquisition | Outcome |
    |---|---|---|
    | Gork Slaughterend, orca raider | First to seize it after crystallization | Bore permanent frostbite and corruption scars. Died reaching for it in the corrupted thermal chambers of ☽'thaw Iasnolica~, killed by the Augur ☽'Milucaum~ Shadow-bane |
    | ☽'ofund' Guild, Nightshelf trade faction | Unknown; held it after Gork's death | Built the shrine Ritus∴um to contain it — from fear, not reverence |
    | At least six unnamed holders | Unrecorded | Unrecorded |
    | Ruibung, orca | Theft during the Mbra∴silentiu, the silent conflict surrounding the artifact | Tried at tribunal before Luminary Xuzhong Pelm'Oath; convicted of trespass and theft; exiled |
    | Rend Kzulrora, orca raider | Stolen from a Nightshelf Augur | Carries it in a body-harness against his right ribs. Has never activated it |
    
    The claim that it passed through "at least six hands" between the Guild's custody and Ruibung's theft appears in multiple accounts but is unsupported by specific names or dates. I note this not to dispute it — six is plausible — but because round numbers in artifact provenance chains warrant the same scrutiny I would apply to round casualty figures.
    
    ## Effects on Holders
    
    Proximity induces involuntary recall of ice-memories not belonging to the holder — a phenomenon the ☽'ofund' Guild miners who guarded Ritus∴um reported consistently. Extended holders develop what sources describe as a specific quality of silence: not peace but the aftermath of having heard something the ice remembers.
    
    Even brief contact exacts a cost. During Ruibung's tribunal, a prosecutor held the artifact for four seconds and set it down with shaking flippers. No bailiff present would touch it afterward.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The Shadow-glass remains with Rend Kzulrora, carried alongside the Nighted~ Lantern. That he has never activated it suggests either unusual restraint or an accurate understanding of what the artifact does to those who engage with it willingly rather than by accident. The ☽'ofund' Guild's claim has not been formally relinquished. The artifact is, as it has always been, fractured between holders who cannot use it and claimants who cannot hold it.
